@section('description')Add Propel and Rocket to Cargo.toml, with Rust stable and Node.js 22.12 or newer for Vite and Tailwind, then render your first view.@endsection
@section('og_type')article@endsection
@section('structured_data')<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","name":"Installation and configuration","url":"https://propel.rocketboost.rs/docs/install","description":"Add Propel and Rocket to Cargo.toml, with Rust stable and Node.js 22.12 or newer for Vite and Tailwind, then render your first view."}</script>@endsection

@section('title')Installation and configuration@endsection

@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Propel is a Rust crate that works with Rocket. It needs a Rust toolchain and, for assets, Node.js.</p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rust, stable, with Cargo.</li>
    <li>Node.js 22.12 or newer, or 20.19 or newer, for Vite and Tailwind.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Add Propel to your project</h2>
  <p>Add Propel and Rocket to <code>Cargo.toml</code>. Use the same Rocket revision as Propel, so the project has one copy of Rocket:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-toml">[dependencies]
rocket = { git = "https://github.com/rwf2/Rocket", rev = "3a54d079" }
propel_rocket = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/Propel", rev = "600a609ae8046604160bbf9837a6258c0c30d04e" }</code></pre>
  <p>Propel is not on crates.io yet, so it comes from git. Pin <code>rev</code> to a commit you have tested.</p>

  <h2>Render a view</h2>
  <p>A route builds the context for its view and returns the rendered page:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">#[get("/posts")]
fn posts() -> PropelView {
    PropelView::render("posts.index", &amp;PostsIndex { posts: all_posts() })
}</code></pre>
  <p>The view name <code>posts.index</code> is the file <code>resources/views/posts/index.propel</code>. Dots are folder separators.</p>

  <h2>Project layout</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-text">your-app/
├── Cargo.toml
├── src/              routes and view contexts
├── resources/
│   ├── views/        .propel templates
│   │   ├── layouts/app.propel
│   │   └── posts/index.propel
│   └── css, js, images, fonts for Vite
├── public/           built files (generated, not committed)
├── package.json
└── vite.config.js</code></pre>
  <p>Views are read from <code>resources/views/</code>, and built files from <code>public/</code>, both relative to the folder you run the app from. There is no configuration file yet.</p>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">npm install
npm run dev     # Vite, in one terminal
cargo run       # the Rocket app, in another</code></pre>

  <h2>Hot reload</h2>
  <p>Edits show up in two ways, and the two cost different amounts of work:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Views</strong> (<code>resources/views/**/*.propel</code>) are compiled into the binary when it builds. Run <code>bacon</code> instead of <code>cargo run</code>: it watches the views too, and it rebuilds and restarts the server on each save.</li>
    <li><strong>Rust code</strong> (<code>src/</code>) needs a rebuild, and <code>bacon</code> does it on each save.</li>
    <li><strong>CSS and JavaScript</strong> come from the Vite dev server, which <code>npm run dev</code> starts. It applies changes without a page reload, and it reloads the page when a view changes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Without <code>npm run dev</code>, the page loads the built files from <code>public/build</code>, so run <code>npm run build</code> first.</p>

  <h2>Release builds</h2>
  <p><code>cargo build --release</code> compiles each view into the binary, through a <code>build.rs</code> file. The binary then needs no <code>resources/views</code> folder. The <code>PropelView::render</code> call that reads views from disk exists in development builds only, so a release app returns each compiled view with <code>PropelView::from_result</code>. The <a target="_new" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/lajennylove/Propel/blob/main/docs/production.md">production guide</a> lists the steps and the caveats.</p>

  <h2>Production</h2>
  <p>Run the deployed app in release mode. A debug build reads each view from disk on every request and shows detailed error pages to visitors. A release build compiles the views into the binary and shows a plain error page. The debug-only demo routes, such as the error pages and the dump examples, are absent from a release build, so they are not served on a production site.</p>

  <h2>Deploying with GitHub Actions</h2>
  <p>Deploy by building in CI and serving on the server: GitHub Actions compiles the release binary and the assets, uploads them with a key that can only write release files, and switches the live release with a second key. The server never compiles, and a failed health check switches the previous release back in. The template, with the workflow, the server scripts and the guide, is in <a target="_new" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/lajennylove/RocketBoost/tree/deploy-template/deploy-template">the deploy-template folder</a> of the RocketBoost repository.</p>

  <p>The three files, copy-ready. The same files are in <a target="_new" rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/lajennylove/RocketBoost/tree/deploy-template/deploy-template">the deploy-template folder</a>.</p>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">.github/workflows/deploy.yml</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-yaml"># GitHub Actions template: build a Rocket app in CI, upload the release to the server,
# and switch to it there. The server only serves files; it never compiles.
#
# Copy to .github/workflows/deploy.yml in your repository and fill in the values marked
# TODO. See README.md in this folder for the one-time server set-up and the secrets.
name: Deploy

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

# One deploy at a time per branch. A running deploy is never cancelled half-way.
concurrency:
  group: deploy-$@{{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

env:
  APP_NAME: my-app          # TODO: the app folder on the server, e.g. ignite-docs
  BIN_NAME: my-app          # TODO: the cargo binary name, as in [[bin]] or the package name
  EXTRA_BINS: &quot;&quot;            # TODO: other binaries the release needs, e.g. &quot;ignite&quot; for MIGRATE_BIN
  DEPLOY_HOST: $@{{ vars.DEPLOY_HOST }}   # repository variable: the server&#x27;s address
  DEPLOY_USER: deploy

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04   # the server runs Ubuntu 24.04; match it so the binary runs there
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@@v4

      - uses: dtolnay/rust-toolchain@@stable
      - uses: Swatinem/rust-cache@@v2

      - name: Node for the front-end assets
        if: hashFiles(&#x27;package.json&#x27;) != &#x27;&#x27;
        uses: actions/setup-node@@v4
        with:
          node-version: 22
          cache: npm

      - name: Build the front-end assets
        if: hashFiles(&#x27;package.json&#x27;) != &#x27;&#x27;
        run: |
          npm ci
          npm run build

      - name: Build the release binary
        run: cargo build --release --locked --bin &quot;$BIN_NAME&quot;

      - name: Stage the release
        run: |
          rm -rf stage &amp;&amp; mkdir stage
          for bin in $BIN_NAME $EXTRA_BINS; do
            cp &quot;target/release/$bin&quot; stage/
          done
          # Everything the app reads at run time goes here, e.g. the built assets.
          if [ -d public ]; then cp -r public stage/; fi
          ls -la stage

      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@@v4
        with:
          name: release
          path: stage
          retention-days: 7

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-24.04
    environment: production   # TODO: create this environment; add reviewers if you want approval
    steps:
      - uses: actions/download-artifact@@v4
        with:
          name: release
          path: stage

      - name: Restore the executable bit
        # Artifact downloads can drop the executable bit, and systemd then fails with 203/EXEC.
        run: |
          for bin in $BIN_NAME $EXTRA_BINS; do
            chmod +x &quot;stage/$bin&quot;
          done

      - name: Install the SSH keys
        run: |
          install -m 700 -d ~/.ssh
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_UPLOAD_KEY }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/upload
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_ACTIVATE_KEY }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/activate
          printf &#x27;%s\n&#x27; &quot;$@{{ secrets.DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS }}&quot; &gt; ~/.ssh/known_hosts
          chmod 600 ~/.ssh/upload ~/.ssh/activate ~/.ssh/known_hosts

      - name: Upload the release
        # The upload key can only write into this app&#x27;s releases folder, under the commit id.
        run: |
          rsync -az -e &quot;ssh -i $HOME/.ssh/upload -o UserKnownHostsFile=$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts&quot; \
            stage/ &quot;$DEPLOY_USER@@$DEPLOY_HOST:$GITHUB_SHA/&quot;

      - name: Activate the release
        # The activation key runs activate.sh for this app. The commit id is the command.
        run: |
          ssh -i &quot;$HOME/.ssh/activate&quot; -o UserKnownHostsFile=&quot;$HOME/.ssh/known_hosts&quot; \
            &quot;$DEPLOY_USER@@$DEPLOY_HOST&quot; &quot;$GITHUB_SHA&quot;</code></pre>
  </details>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">activate.sh, on the server at /home/deploy/bin/activate.sh</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
# Activates one uploaded release of one app. Runs on the server only, through the
# forced command of the activation key in authorized_keys, which fixes the app name:
#
#   command=&quot;/home/deploy/bin/activate.sh &lt;app&gt;&quot;,restrict  ssh-ed25519 AAAA... ci-activate
#
# The client sends the release id as the command (SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND). The release is
# switched in atomically with a symlink. If the app is not healthy afterwards, the
# previous release is switched back in. See deploy-template/README.md.
set -euo pipefail

APP=&quot;${1:?the app name is fixed by the forced command}&quot;
RELEASE=&quot;${SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND:-}&quot;
if [[ ! &quot;$RELEASE&quot; =~ ^[0-9a-f]{40}$ ]]; then
  echo &quot;activate: the release id must be a full 40-character commit id&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  exit 2
fi

APP_DIR=&quot;$HOME/apps/$APP&quot;
# shellcheck source=/dev/null
. &quot;$APP_DIR/shared/deploy.env&quot;   # SERVICE, HEALTH_URL, KEEP, and optionally MIGRATE_BIN
REL_DIR=&quot;$APP_DIR/releases/$RELEASE&quot;
if [ ! -d &quot;$REL_DIR&quot; ]; then
  echo &quot;activate: release $RELEASE was not uploaded&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  exit 2
fi

PREVIOUS=&quot;$(readlink &quot;$APP_DIR/current&quot; || true)&quot;

# Migrations run before the switch, as a deploy step (Laravel&#x27;s `migrate`).
if [ -n &quot;${MIGRATE_BIN:-}&quot; ]; then
  set -a
  # shellcheck source=/dev/null
  . &quot;$APP_DIR/shared/.env&quot;
  set +a
  &quot;$REL_DIR/$MIGRATE_BIN&quot; migrate
fi

switch() {
  ln -sfn &quot;$1&quot; &quot;$APP_DIR/current.new&quot;
  mv -Tf &quot;$APP_DIR/current.new&quot; &quot;$APP_DIR/current&quot;
  sudo /bin/systemctl restart &quot;$SERVICE&quot;
}

healthy() {
  for _ in $(seq 1 20); do
    if curl -fsS -o /dev/null --max-time 3 &quot;$HEALTH_URL&quot;; then
      return 0
    fi
    sleep 1
  done
  return 1
}

switch &quot;$REL_DIR&quot;
if ! healthy; then
  echo &quot;activate: $RELEASE is not healthy at $HEALTH_URL&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  if [ -n &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot; ] &amp;&amp; [ -d &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot; ]; then
    switch &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot;
    healthy || true
    echo &quot;activate: rolled back to $(basename &quot;$PREVIOUS&quot;)&quot; &gt;&amp;2
  fi
  exit 1
fi

# Keep the newest releases for rollback and delete the rest.
ls -1dt &quot;$APP_DIR&quot;/releases/*/ | tail -n +&quot;$((KEEP + 1))&quot; | xargs -r rm -rf
echo &quot;live $RELEASE&quot;</code></pre>
  </details>
  <details class="docs-details my-6">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-heading">app.service, the systemd unit</summary>
    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash"># systemd unit for one app. Copy to /etc/systemd/system/&lt;service&gt;.service, replacing
# the placeholders in angle brackets, then run: sudo systemctl daemon-reload
# The app runs from the `current` symlink, which activate.sh switches between releases.
[Unit]
Description=&lt;App name&gt; (Rocket)
After=network.target

[Service]
User=deploy
Group=deploy
WorkingDirectory=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/current
EnvironmentFile=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/shared/.env
Environment=ROCKET_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
Environment=ROCKET_PORT=&lt;port&gt;
ExecStart=/home/deploy/apps/&lt;app&gt;/current/&lt;binary&gt;
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>
  </details>

  <h2>A note on the example app</h2>
  <p>This site is the docs app in the <code>RocketPropel</code> repository, and its layout is the one this page shows. The navigation and the page wrapper that adds it to each view are part of the app for now. Propel will provide them later.</p>
@endsection
